**Claim:** The sequence $x_n = (n+i\pi)^n\, n^{-n+1/n}$ converges, and its limit is $-1$.

**Proof.**

Factor $n$ out of the first factor:

$$n + i\pi = n\!\left(1 + \frac{i\pi}{n}\right).$$

Hence

$$(n+i\pi)^n = n^n\!\left(1+\frac{i\pi}{n}\right)^{\!n}.$$

Also,

$$n^{-n+1/n} = n^{-n}\cdot n^{1/n}.$$

Therefore

$$x_n = n^n\!\left(1+\frac{i\pi}{n}\right)^{\!n}\cdot n^{-n}\cdot n^{1/n}
      = \left(1+\frac{i\pi}{n}\right)^{\!n}\cdot n^{1/n}.$$

Now examine each factor separately.

**First factor.** For real $t\to 0$, the classical limit $(1+t)^{1/t}\to e$ holds, and more generally $(1+z_n)^{n}\to e^{z}$ whenever $z_n\to z$ (this follows, e.g., from $\log(1+w)=w+O(w^2)$ for $|w|$ small, giving $n\log(1+i\pi/n)=i\pi+O(1/n)\to i\pi$). With $z_n = i\pi/n\to 0$,

$$\left(1+\frac{i\pi}{n}\right)^{\!n} \longrightarrow e^{i\pi} = -1.$$

**Second factor.** The standard result $\log(n^{1/n}) = \frac{\log n}{n}\to 0$ gives

$$n^{1/n}\longrightarrow 1.$$

**Combining.** Both limits exist, so by the product rule for convergent sequences,

$$\lim_{n\to\infty} x_n = \left(\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1+\frac{i\pi}{n}\right)^{\!n}\right)\!\left(\lim_{n\to\infty} n^{1/n}\right) = (-1)\cdot 1 = -1.$$

Thus the sequence converges, and

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty} x_n = -1.}$$

